hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1 has "resemblance_common ∧ concur → principle_proved"; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.2 has "external_enliven ∧ types_present ∧ sensible_influence ∧ convey_related → resemblance_common" and "resemblance_common ∧ concur → principle_proved"; they differ when only "the external motions, postures and actions of the ceremonies enliven devotion and quicken fervour, which otherwise would decay if directed entirely to distant and immaterial objects", "shadowing out the objects of faith in sensible types and images renders them more present than is possible by a merely intellectual view and contemplation", "sensible objects have always a greater influence on the fancy than any other" and "sensible objects readily convey this influence to those ideas to which they are related and which they resemble" are true, which the first allows; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it [in 1 of 2 runs]
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.2 take for granted "if (the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common and in every case a resemblance and a present impression must concur), then we are abundantly supplied with experiments to prove the reality of the foregoing principle"; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.3 does not [in 1 of 2 runs]
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common and in every case a resemblance and a present impression must concur), then we are abundantly supplied with experiments to prove the reality of the foregoing principle"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 1 of 2 runs]
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.2 takes for granted "if (the external motions, postures and actions of the ceremonies enliven devotion and quicken fervour, which otherwise would decay if directed entirely to distant and immaterial objects and shadowing out the objects of faith in sensible types and images renders them more present than is possible by a merely intellectual view and contemplation and sensible objects have always a greater influence on the fancy than any other and sensible objects readily convey this influence to those ideas to which they are related and which they resemble), then the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common"; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.3 do not [in 1 of 2 runs]
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the external motions, postures and actions of the ceremonies enliven devotion and quicken fervour, which otherwise would decay if directed entirely to distant and immaterial objects and shadowing out the objects of faith in sensible types and images renders them more present than is possible by a merely intellectual view and contemplation and sensible objects have always a greater influence on the fancy than any other and sensible objects readily convey this influence to those ideas to which they are related and which they resemble), then the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 1 of 2 runs]
